we turn now to something called the  wagner group its innocuous sounding name  belies the ugly truth about the wagner  group a collection of russian  mercenaries with a track record of  brutality and war crimes now dispatched  by vladimir putin to ukraine  that's right brian little is known about  them except they are believed to be one  of putin's secret weapons in his efforts  to inflict pain on civilians  and they are being closely watched by  u.s officials  it would be  wrong to say that  that  by using foreign fighters we have sort  of greater worries about brutality  because the russian forces themselves  have been  uh brutal uh in some of the worst ways  uh but it is i think a statement of mr  putin's desire to to bolster his  manpower  in response to the depletion of that  manpower over the last six weeks  to get a better understanding of the  secretive group we're joined now by  jason blizekis a senior research fellow  at the safan group thank you so much for  being here jason why are they called the  wagner group for starters  so they were founded about a decade ago  by an individual by the name of dmitry  utkin and dimitri luken is an individual  who used the call sign wagner and is an  individual who had a great love for  the uh uh the most uh virulent of of  nazis adolf hitler and adolf hitler's  favorite composer was richard wagner so  who can took the moniker wagner group to  name his private military company that  um and there is this neo-nazi connection  dust to the wagner group and they are  very active throughout the globe sadly  and we've seen evidence of that across  the world really of their brutality  we see it in ukraine right now um there  are reports from the united nations that  go back nearly two years ago that the  organization was involved in heinous  human rights abuses in the central  african republic and just a few days ago  um there are reports that the wagner  group was engaged in a false flag  operation that led to the deaths of  individuals in mali and they were  creating a grave trying to blame the  french military for these deaths of  these individuals  likely at the hands of the wagner group  jason a question for you from rhonda  schwartz rhonda  in your new research report you also  talk about the role of foreign fighters  from the west what have you learned  about the role that they're playing who  are those folks  so the russia ukraine conflict has  become a crucible for  melting pot if you will of individuals  with far-right sympathies who are  flocking to join the fight in some cases  to join the fight uh with the the  russians in some cases these are  individuals that are from russia going  into ukraine like the russian imperial  movement a far-right  terrorist group that was sanctioned by  the u.s department of state in 2020 in  some other cases you have had reports of  individuals from the united states  trying to join the fight in ukraine um  in some cases we have seen reports that  the so-called boogaloo boys um have  tried to go to ukraine to fight i think  in those reports they they were  discouraged by what they saw it and came  back essentially what you see is uh  nazis uh far-right individuals uh  primarily joining the russian side but  you do have some reports of individuals  joining the ukrainian side that may have  far-right sympathies as well so you see  upwards to perhaps uh thousands of  individuals making the travel the  pilgrimage if you will to to  ukraine to fight um precise numbers are  unknown but it's very uh disturbing to  see that the fight of foreign uh fire  fighters in fact there's even reports  that the russians are trying to bring  individuals from syria and iraq who have  uh battle-hardened skills to join the  fight on behalf of the russians paying  upwards to two thousand dollars per  individual so very disturbing reports  that we're seeing and jason given the  size of the russian army why does putin  need to dispatch the wagner group  so there are reports uh recent ones in  fact that there might be upwards to 8  000 members of the wagner group there in  ukraine and part of the reason being  that this provides some plausible  deniability to the russian government  the wagner group technically is not part  of the russian government but has been  used by the russians as a tool of their  foreign policy to give them this era of  aura of deniability essentially and in  this context they can go out and  oftentimes go to places not necessarily  being uniformed and do some really bad  things they can engage in reconnaissance  to provide information to the russian  conventional forces they can engage in  human rights abuses to target civilians  to try to get information from those  individuals um and generally cause chaos  and engage in brutality in an effort to  cow the ukrainian population essentially  to bend to the russian will  there are many other things that they're  involved in of course but these are just  some of the low lights and does their  command structure go up to the russian  army are they under the control of some  army  so the interesting thing about the  wagner group is that they are funded by  a close henchman of vladimir putin by  the individual by the name of yageni um  pergosian and pragosian is also known as  putin's uh chef he bankrolls the  organization so you can believe that  this is an organization absolutely that  is operating at the behest of the  russian federation at the perhaps the  highest levels  and they continue to be there in ukraine  now as best you know  they are and there was recent reports in  fact by a think tank that goes by the  name bellingcat that said that upwards  to three thousand members of the wagner  group have been killed and the wagner  group's also useful for the russian  federation because it could take  essentially the the death count and make  it lower for vladimir putin instead of  having russian conscripts and russian  military forces dying you have this  deniable force that's dying on behalf of  the russian federation and they don't  have to count towards the body count so  they're also useful for trying to keep  the russian domestic population happy  about this conflict is it clear that  putin though endorses their use and  enjoys having them on the field of  battle for himself  well he hasn't been explicit about that  but the fact that they're there that  they're operating in areas of the the  east and southern portions of ukraine  they were even up in the north portions  towards kiev um all of these uh are  examples that uh putin is allowing for  them to operate you know perhaps not  explicitly they have his implicit  approval all right jason vlazekis from  the soufon center thank you so much for  being with us here tonight  thank you brian you bet coming up next  this week's winners and losers in the  media see if you agree with the choices  made by the editors of mediaite  you're watching brian ross investigates  on the long crime trial network  time now for this week's winners and  losers in the media as chosen by the  editors of mediaite and we're joined by  aidan mclaughlin who is the  editor-in-chief of mediaite which like  the law and crime trial network is part  of the dan abrams media group and aiden  you've chosen as the winner this week  clarissa ward from cnn with incredible  reporting from the battlefield in  ukraine  all right  is everybody okay alexandra asks  our team member maria avdiva has cut up  her hands on broken glass  vladimir treats her injuries as  alexandra calls the dispatch again to  find where the wounded are  you got no connection we're sitting in  the entrance she says  and they're shelling the shit out of us  well aidan reporting from the front  lines uh incredible job by clarissa ward  right and this is what we know clarissa  ward for she's a cnn senior  international correspondent she's  reported from war zones all across the  world she uh is currently in kharkiv in  ukraine um which is the subject of heavy  russian fire um and did this report  where she basically her and her crew the  cnn crew trailed uh ukrainian paramedics  as they went into places that had been  bombed by russia by the russian military  and tried to rescue victims on the  ground um now the report delved into a  russian tactic known as doubletap  striking which is where russian  artillery bombs a place and then the the  planes circle around wait for the  paramedics to run in and then drop  another bomb  which is a pretty horrific  strategy for war that was used in syria  a lot when when russia was helping the  assad regime fight there  but it was truly impressive brave  reporting from clarissa ward and her cnn  team  they actually had to dodge fire at one  point as you saw in that video  it's what we've come to expect from  curse award but it makes it no less  impressive indeed  and for a loser this week you've chosen  former congressperson  devin nunes who left congress to become  part of the donald trump social media  outlet which hasn't done so well here's  how he tried to justify it  twitter right now is nothing but a pr  wire uh it's it's got a global footprint  but there's just nobody there and i mean  and how do you explain  how we have more engagement on true  social than they have on twitter  a brave front there but what's the  problem for him right so that claim that  truth social has had more engagement  than twitter um most people have uh sort  of dismissed that as patently false um  true social is uh the social media  platform that got launched by trump uh  to compete with twitter devin nunes left  congress to join truth social as its ceo  now the launch of truth social uh which  came a couple weeks ago uh was  marred by a bunch of technical issues uh  at one point there was a wait list that  was said to have been of a million  people all trying to get on the platform  uh we had a reporter actually that got  on truth social recently  and wrote about uh for mediaite all of  the various problems with it  one big problem donald trump doesn't  even use truth social um donald trump  has yet to post on it or he posted a  couple times when it first came out has  not posted on it since february um so  trump isn't even using his own platform  uh he says that he's not going to be  coming back to twitter even if elon musk  restores his account that he's gonna be  staying with truth social um but elon  musk is now going after truth social he  said uh on twitter today uh that it has  a terrible name and he actually made a  good point he said it only exists uh  because twitter he said censored free  speech and that's a good point in the  problem for truth social and devin nunes  going forward it's that without twitter  being considered to be since  cracking down on conservatives there's  no reason for truth social and if  conservatives see twitter as being saved  by elon musk then they're going to  return to that social media platform  they're not going to use something like  truth social which does not have the  audience and doesn't have the user  experience that twitter has so in short  elon musk may doom truth social you know  that's what people are saying and uh in  fact the company that truth social is  trying to uh go public with that they  made a spec with um  which is trading publicly uh their  stocks dropped 50 percent uh since march  so i think it's clear that investors at  least do not see truth social as a  viable social media platform going  forward and you certainly have chosen a  good loser and devin nunes given all the  events that are surrounding truth social  and twitter aiden thank you so much for  being here and thanks to all of you for  watching we'll see you again back here  next week on the law and crime trial  network  [Applause]  you

